In article <HAIRSTON.90Apr25222123@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
[Lots of good stuff, conspicuously ignoring an excellent suggestion
about requiring confirmation before attacking an optinal list of monsters.
Then a suggestion to turn off the safepet option in order to attack one's
pet.]
As far as I can tell the safepet option is entirely useless in a game
with alignments, because you would prompted anyway before attacking
peaceful or tame monsters (regardless of alignment, incidentally).  In
fact, the safepet option is odious and vile because it wastes a turn
in which nearby monsters (or your pet, if confused) can beat on you,
not to mention wasting your food.  Why does it do this?  Why is it a
default option?  Why does it exist at all?

